    "report": "The message notice now carries `listingId`, and the work is committed on `main` as `2563402`. Nothing is pushed. Typecheck, lint, and Prettier are clean, and the full Jest suite passes (269 suites, 1656 tests). The message-notice e2e specs passed on their own; the full-suite run had one failing spec, which I fixed (below).\n\n**What changed**\n- **Messaging:** `MessageSentEvent` and the `MessageSentPayload` integration payload now carry `listingId`. The conversation already knew its listing, so it is passed through from there.\n- **Notifications:** `MessageNoticeBody` gains `listingId`. `MessageNoticeOwing` and the sent-message handler pass it through. It is stored in the existing `listing_id` column, which calendar conflicts already use, so there is no migration.\n- **Unchanged:** who is told, the window, delivery, retries, and the other notice kinds.\n- **Tests:** I updated the fixtures and the exact-body assertions in the notification specs, the message-notice e2e spec, and `messageClock.routes.e2e.spec.ts`. That last one was the failure in the full run: it pins the whole notice body, and the new field is the intended change.\n\n**Things you should know**\n- **Notices already owed before deploy have no listing id.** Their rows have `listing_id` NULL, and they will be sent with `listingId: null` even though the type says `string`. Fixing this needs a backfill from the conversations table, which means a cross-context read. I did not write one. Say if you want it.\n- **Docs not updated.** ADR 57 describes the sent-message event's fields, and it now omits the listing. I left the ADR alone because it is a decision record. `conventions/storms/message-notices.yaml` lists event names only, so it needed no change.\n- **Port 4471 not used.** The e2e specs bind their own ephemeral ports, so I did not start a server.",
